{
 "id": "ros-packages",
 "check": {
  "task": [
   {
    "t": "p",
    "c": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "In two or three sentences each, and without notes: what is a ROS 2 workspace, what is an overlay, and what is "
     },
     {
      "t": "code",
      "v": "package.xml"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " for? Take 10 minutes."
     }
    ]
   }
  ],
  "answer": [
   {
    "t": "p",
    "c": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "A workspace is a folder with a "
     },
     {
      "t": "code",
      "v": "src"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " directory of packages. "
     },
     {
      "t": "code",
      "v": "colcon build"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": ", run at its top, builds every package into "
     },
     {
      "t": "code",
      "v": "build"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": ", installs the results into "
     },
     {
      "t": "code",
      "v": "install"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " and writes logs to "
     },
     {
      "t": "code",
      "v": "log"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "t": "p",
    "c": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "An overlay is a workspace whose "
     },
     {
      "t": "code",
      "v": "install/setup.bash"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " you source on top of another environment, the underlay, which is usually "
     },
     {
      "t": "code",
      "v": "/opt/ros/jazzy"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": ". The overlay's packages are then found first, so your own version of a package replaces the one underneath."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "t": "p",
    "c": [
     {
      "t": "code",
      "v": "package.xml"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " is the package manifest. It gives the name, version, license and build type, and lists the dependencies. colcon uses those dependencies to build packages in the right order, and rosdep uses them to install system packages."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "t": "p",
    "c": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "You pass with all three, including the idea that the overlay is found first. Missing one is a partial pass, and missing two is a fail."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
 "interview": [
  {
   "t": "p",
   "c": [
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": "A ROS 2 workspace is a folder holding a "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "src"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": " directory of packages, each package a unit of code with its own "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "package.xml"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": " manifest naming its version, license, build type and dependencies. "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "colcon build"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": ", run at the top of the workspace, finds every "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "package.xml"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": " under "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "src"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": ", reads the dependencies to decide what to build first, and puts the results in a "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "build"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": " directory, the installed files in an "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "install"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": " directory, and its logs in "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "log"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": "."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "p",
   "c": [
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": "The problem is that a machine running ROS 2 usually has more than one copy of the same package available, a system install such as "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "/opt/ros/jazzy"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": " and whatever you have built yourself, and something has to decide which one a command such as "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "ros2 run"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": " actually finds, as well as which order dependent packages get built in."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "p",
   "c": [
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": "The mechanism is sourcing. "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": " sets environment variables such as "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "AMENT_PREFIX_PATH"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": ", "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "PATH"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": " and "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "LD_LIBRARY_PATH"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": " so that tools and libraries under that prefix are found; that installation is the "
    },
    {
     "t": "i",
     "v": "underlay"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": ". Sourcing a workspace's own "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "install/setup.bash"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": " adds its install folder in front of the underlay's in those same variables, which makes the workspace an "
    },
    {
     "t": "i",
     "v": "overlay"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": ", and a package that exists in both is taken from the overlay because its path comes first. Building writes files into "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "install"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": ", but nothing looks there until an "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "install/setup.bash"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": " is sourced."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "p",
   "c": [
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": "What trips people up is that a fresh terminal starts from "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": ".bashrc"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": " alone, so a workspace built in one terminal stays invisible in another until its "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "install/setup.bash"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": " is sourced there too, and "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "Package not found"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": " from something that clearly built looks like a build failure when it is really a missing source. The other trap is a stale "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "install"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": ": colcon does not remove a file it no longer produces, so renaming a node, a launch file or a package can leave the old binary running under its old name, unnoticed until "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "build"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": " and "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "install"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": " are deleted and rebuilt."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "p",
   "c": [
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": "This is the interview question itself: saying what a workspace, an overlay and "
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "v": "package.xml"
    },
    {
     "t": "text",
     "v": " are for is the first vocabulary this whole track assumes, because every later page hands you a package to build, source and run."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "subs": [
  {
   "id": "workspace",
   "title": "Workspaces, overlays, ament_cmake, package.xml, colcon",
   "steps": [
    {
     "kind": "theory",
     "heading": "Theory",
     "minutes": 20,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": "Create a workspace with one package and build it:"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "sh",
       "tag": "",
       "expect": "",
       "text": "mkdir -p ~/demo_ws/src && cd ~/demo_ws/src\nros2 pkg create --build-type ament_cmake demo_pkg --dependencies rclcpp\ncd ~/demo_ws\ncolcon build\nls                                  # build  install  log  src\nros2 pkg prefix demo_pkg            # Package not found\nsource install/setup.bash\nros2 pkg prefix demo_pkg            # /home/you/demo_ws/install/demo_pkg"
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": "colcon found "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "demo_pkg"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " by looking for "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "package.xml"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " files under "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "src"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": ". It read the dependencies to decide the build order, and ran CMake for it because the manifest says its build type is "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "ament_cmake"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": ". The results went into "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "install/demo_pkg"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": ". Then "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "ros2 pkg prefix"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " couldn't find the package, even though it was built. That is the point most people miss at first: building doesn't make a package visible. Sourcing does."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " sets environment variables such as "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "AMENT_PREFIX_PATH"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": ", "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "PATH"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " and "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "LD_LIBRARY_PATH"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " so that tools and libraries under "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "/opt/ros/jazzy"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " are found. Sourcing "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "install/setup.bash"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " of your workspace adds your install folder in front of those paths. Your workspace is now an "
        },
        {
         "t": "i",
         "v": "overlay"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " on top of the "
        },
        {
         "t": "i",
         "v": "underlay"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "/opt/ros/jazzy"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": ", and a package that exists in both is taken from the overlay. The underlay has to be sourced before you build, because CMake finds "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "rclcpp"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " through it. The overlay has to be sourced in every terminal where you want to run what you built."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "package.xml"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " lists dependencies by kind. "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "<depend>"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " means needed to build and to run, "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "<build_depend>"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " and "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "<exec_depend>"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " split the two, and "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "<test_depend>"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " is only for tests. From the manifest, "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "rosdep install --from-paths src --ignore-src -y"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " installs the system packages a workspace needs. That is how a fresh machine gets from a clone to a build."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": "In an ament_cmake package, "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "CMakeLists.txt"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " finds each dependency with "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "find_package"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": ", builds targets, and installs them. For an executable, "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "install(TARGETS my_node DESTINATION lib/${PROJECT_NAME})"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " is what makes "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "ros2 run my_package my_node"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " work. It ends with "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "ament_package()"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": ", which writes the files other packages need to find this one."
        }
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "example",
     "heading": "Worked example",
     "minutes": 15,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "ros2 pkg create"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " generated these two files. Here is "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "package.xml"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": ", with the maintainer shown as placeholders:"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "xml",
       "tag": "",
       "expect": "",
       "text": "<?xml version=\"1.0\"?>\n<package format=\"3\">\n  <name>demo_pkg</name>\n  <version>0.0.0</version>\n  <description>TODO: Package description</description>\n  <maintainer email=\"you@example.com\">you</maintainer>\n  <license>TODO: License declaration</license>\n\n  <buildtool_depend>ament_cmake</buildtool_depend>\n\n  <depend>rclcpp</depend>\n\n  <test_depend>ament_lint_auto</test_depend>\n  <test_depend>ament_lint_common</test_depend>\n\n  <export>\n    <build_type>ament_cmake</build_type>\n  </export>\n</package>"
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": "Notice three things. "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "rclcpp"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " is a "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "<depend>"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": ", because the "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "--dependencies"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " option put it there. "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "ament_cmake"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " is a build tool dependency, not a library one. And the build type sits under "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "<export>"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": ", which is where colcon reads it from."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "CMakeLists.txt"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": ", with its comments and lint setup trimmed:"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "cmake",
       "tag": "",
       "expect": "",
       "text": "cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.8)\nproject(demo_pkg)\n\nif(CMAKE_COMPILER_IS_GNUCXX OR CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID MATCHES \"Clang\")\n  add_compile_options(-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic)\nendif()\n\nfind_package(ament_cmake REQUIRED)\nfind_package(rclcpp REQUIRED)\n\nament_package()"
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": "It builds nothing yet. To add a node, add three lines before "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "ament_package()"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": ", and colcon rebuilds only what changed:"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "cmake",
       "tag": "",
       "expect": "",
       "text": "add_executable(hello src/hello.cpp)\nament_target_dependencies(hello rclcpp)\ninstall(TARGETS hello DESTINATION lib/${PROJECT_NAME})"
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "ament_target_dependencies"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " is the Jazzy tutorials' way to link a target against ROS packages. Newer distributions are moving to plain "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "target_link_libraries"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " with targets such as "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "rclcpp::rclcpp"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": ", which also works in Jazzy."
        }
       ]
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "package",
   "title": "Create a package with a dependency and a custom message",
   "steps": [
    {
     "kind": "exercise",
     "heading": "Exercise",
     "minutes": 30,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": "Take 30 minutes. Messages usually live in a package of their own, so that other packages can depend on them without depending on your nodes."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "ol",
       "items": [
        [
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": "In "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "~/bench_ws/src"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": ", create an ament_cmake package "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "bench_interfaces"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": "."
         }
        ],
        [
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": "Add a message "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "msg/Tick.msg"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " with two fields: an "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "int64 count"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " and a "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "builtin_interfaces/Time stamp"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": "."
         }
        ],
        [
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": "Make the package generate the message code, then build it with colcon."
         }
        ],
        [
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": "Source the workspace and print the message definition with "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "ros2 interface show bench_interfaces/msg/Tick"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": "."
         }
        ],
        [
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": "Write down the header you would include in C++ and the name of the class."
         }
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": "The official tutorial on "
        },
        {
         "t": "a",
         "v": "custom interfaces",
         "href": "https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials/Beginner-Client-Libraries/Custom-ROS2-Interfaces.html"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " covers the same steps if you get stuck."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": "Solution"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": "The message file is two lines:"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "text",
       "tag": "",
       "expect": "",
       "text": "int64 count\nbuiltin_interfaces/Time stamp"
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "CMakeLists.txt"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " asks the ROS interface generators to produce the code, and names the message's own dependency:"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "cmake",
       "tag": "",
       "expect": "",
       "text": "cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.8)\nproject(bench_interfaces)\n\nfind_package(ament_cmake REQUIRED)\nfind_package(rosidl_default_generators REQUIRED)\nfind_package(builtin_interfaces REQUIRED)\n\nrosidl_generate_interfaces(${PROJECT_NAME}\n  \"msg/Tick.msg\"\n  DEPENDENCIES builtin_interfaces\n)\n\nament_package()"
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "package.xml"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " needs four lines on top of what "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "ros2 pkg create"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " wrote: the generators at build time, the runtime support, the dependency of the message itself, and membership of the interface packages group:"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "xml",
