  {
   "id": "traps",
   "title": "Forgetting to source; stale builds",
   "steps": [
    {
     "kind": "failure",
     "heading": "Failure cases",
     "minutes": 10,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "Package 'bench_count' not found"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " in a new terminal almost always means the workspace isn't sourced there. Each terminal starts from whatever "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": ".bashrc"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " sets up. Putting "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " in "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": ".bashrc"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " is common. Putting your workspace there as well is riskier, because every terminal then runs your last build, including in the terminal where you are rebuilding it."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": "A deleted executable can keep running. colcon doesn't remove files from "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "install"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " that a new build no longer produces, so after you rename a node, the old name still starts the old binary. The same goes for a removed launch file or a renamed package. When the install folder no longer matches the sources, delete "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "build"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " and "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "install"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " for that package, or for the whole workspace, and build again."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": "If you delete "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "install"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " in a terminal where it was sourced, colcon prints warnings that paths in "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "AMENT_PREFIX_PATH"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " don't exist. The environment still points at the old folder. Open a new terminal, source only the underlay, and build there."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": "A package with the same name in your overlay and in "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "/opt/ros/jazzy"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " is taken from the overlay. That is how you test a fix to a ROS package, and also how you end up running a stale copy by mistake. "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "ros2 pkg prefix <name>"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " tells you which one is used."
        }
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "summary",
     "heading": "Summary",
     "minutes": 10,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": "Write five to eight lines from memory, and keep them as the proof for this topic. They should cover what "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "colcon build"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " produces and where, why a built package isn't visible until you source the overlay, and what "
        },
        {
         "t": "code",
         "v": "package.xml"
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": " lists and who reads it. Name two ways an install folder goes stale, and the fix."
        }
       ]
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
